
{"id":"b1e10310-f294-4e61-9df3-30a5511cbc1b","object":"chat.completion","created":1785027948,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    queue = [n['id'] for n in sorted_nodes]\\n    pending = set(queue)\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.pop(0)\\n        pending.discard(nid)\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        node = nodes[nid]\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        children = [c for c in nodes.values() if c['parent'] == nid and c['id'] in retained]\\n        is_leaf = len(children) == 0\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and is_leaf:\\n            retained.remove(nid)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id in retained and parent_id not in pending:\\n                pending.add(parent_id)\\n                queue.append(parent_id)\\n    return {'retained_node_ids': sorted(list(retained)), 'visit_order': visit_order}\"}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) for task SE-PE-02 based on the given fixture and description. The task is about parse tree reduction: maintain a queue of nodes, process removable leaves with utility below threshold, remove them, and enqueue parent for reconsideration. The output should have retained_node_ids (IDs of nodes that remain after removal) and visit_order (order in which nodes were visited/processed).\n\nThe input payload has nodes list with id, parent, symbol, depth, removable, utility. Also utility_threshold. We need to simulate the process.\n\nLet's outline algorithm:\n\n1. Build a map from id to node (dictionary). Also keep track of parent-child relationships. Initially, all nodes are present. We'll have a set of retained_node_ids initially all node ids. We'll also maintain a queue (list) for processing order.\n\n2. Initialize queue: nodes sorted by descending depth, then stable node-ID order (alphabetical? The description says \"stable node-ID order\". Probably sort by depth descending, then by id ascending (string). Since IDs are strings like \"root\", \"n1\", etc. we can use natural string sort.\n\n3. Process queue one by one. For each node id popped from queue:\n   - Check if node is still retained (not removed). If not, skip.\n   - Append id to visit_order.\n   - If node is removable and utility <= utility_threshold and node is a leaf (i.e., has no children in the current retained tree? Actually leaf means no children that are retained. Initially children may exist but after removals, some children might be removed. We need to check if node has any retained children. If it has children still present, it's not a leaf.) Then remove it: mark as removed (remove from retained set), and enqueue its parent (if parent exists and is retained) for reconsideration.\n   - If node is not removable or utility > threshold or not leaf, do nothing else.\n\n4. Continue until queue empty.\n\nEdge cases: root is not removable (removable: false). The root will never be removed. Also if parent is already queued or processed, we enqueue it again? The description says \"enqueue its retained parent for reconsideration\". We should add parent to queue if it is retained. But if parent is already in queue later, we can add again? Typically we add to the end. The order: we are processing in queue order. When we enqueue parent, it will be processed later. Should we avoid duplicates? We can allow duplicates but we can check when popping if already removed. Safer to use a set to track queued ids to avoid infinite loops. But the description doesn't specify duplicate handling. Usually for BFS/queue, we can add again and skip if already removed. But to be safe, we can check if parent is already in queue (or has been processed) and skip adding if already queued? Let's think. The parent might be queued again after being processed? The parent could have been processed earlier and then later a child removal triggers re-enqueue. That is needed because removal of child might make parent a leaf, so parent should be reconsidered. So we must enqueue even if previously processed. So we should not prevent re-queue. But we should avoid multiple enqueues of same parent from different children? Possibly. The queue will contain duplicates but that's fine as long as we skip if already removed. However to avoid infinite loops, we only enqueue if the parent is still retained. Also if parent is already in the queue (not yet processed), adding another copy is okay; when popped, first instance will process, second will skip because node might have been removed or already processed? Actually after first processing, if node is not removed (e.g., not leaf), second processing will do nothing. Could cause extra work but acceptable. To be efficient, we could track if node is already in queue using a set, but not strictly required. I'll use a simple queue (list) and a set for \"enqueued\" to avoid duplicates to be safe. But careful: if we re-enqueue parent after it was already popped, we need to allow new enqueue. So we can use a set that tracks if node is currently pending (in queue). When we pop, we remove from pending set. When we enqueue, we add to pending set only if not already pending. That prevents duplicates. But if parent is processed, then later a child removal triggers re-enqueue, parent is not pending, so it will be added. That's correct.\n\nLet's implement:\n\n- retained_ids: set of all ids initially.\n- queue: list for processing order. Initialize with sorted nodes by depth descending, then id ascending. For stable order, sort by (-depth, id).\n- pending: set of ids currently in queue (to avoid duplicates).\n- visit_order: list.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\n    # initial queue order\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\n    queue = [n['id'] for n in sorted_nodes]\n    pending = set(queue)\n    visit_order = []\n    while queue:\n        nid = queue.pop(0)  # or use collections.deque for efficiency but standard library fine\n        pending.discard(nid)\n        if nid not in retained:\n            continue\n        node = nodes[nid]\n        visit_order.append(nid)\n        # check if leaf: no retained children\n        # need to find children of node that are retained.\n        # We can precompute children mapping, but need to update as children removed.\n        # Easiest: each time check all nodes to see if parent matches? inefficient but given small input OK.\n        # Better: build adjacency list mapping parent to list of children ids. Update when child removed.\n        children = [c for c in nodes.values() if c['parent'] == nid and c['id'] in retained]\n        is_leaf = len(children) == 0\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and is_leaf:\n            # remove node\n            retained.remove(nid)\n            # enqueue parent\n            parent_id = node['parent']\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id in retained and parent_id not in pending:\n                pending.add(parent_id)\n                queue.append(parent_id)\n    # After processing, retained_ids are the remaining nodes.\n    # visit_order is the order we visited nodes (including removed ones? The description says \"complete visit order\". We appended all nodes we popped from queue even if not removed. That seems correct: we visit each node once.\n    return {\"retained_node_ids\": sorted(list(retained)), \"visit_order\": visit_order}\n\nWait: It says \"Return retained IDs and the complete visit order.\" The retained_node_ids should be the IDs of nodes that are retained (not removed). Should they be sorted? Probably not required, but can be any order. We'll sort for consistency. The visit_order is the order we processed.\n\nCheck first case: root depth0, n1 depth1, n2 depth1, n3 depth1, then n4 depth2 parent n2, n5 depth2 parent n3, n6 depth2 parent n1.\n\nInitial queue sorted by depth descending then id: depth2 nodes: n4, n5, n6 (alphabetically n4, n5, n6). depth1: n1, n2, n3 (alphabetically? n1 < n2 < n3). depth0: root. So queue = [n4, n5, n6, n1, n2, n3, root].\n\nProcess n4: removable=true, utility=0.2087 <=0.55, leaf? children of n4? none, so leaf yes. Remove n4. Enqueue parent n2 (if not pending). n2 not pending (since not in queue yet? Actually n2 is in queue later but not yet pending? pending currently has all initial queue ids. n2 is in pending because it's in queue. So we should not add duplicate. So no addition. Visit_order: [n4].\n\nProcess n5: removable=false, so skip removal. Visit_order: [n4, n5].\n\nProcess n6: removable=true, utility=0.3524 <=0.55, leaf? children of n6? none, so leaf. Remove n6. Enqueue parent n1 (pending? n1 is in queue later, currently pending, so no addition). Visit_order: [n4,n5,n6].\n\nProcess n1: removable=false, skip. Visit_order: [n4,n5,n6,n1].\n\nProcess n2: removable=true, utility=0.7422 >0.55, so no removal. However is it leaf? After removal of n4, n2 has no retained children? n2 originally had n4 as child, now n4 removed. So n2 becomes leaf. But we are processing n2 now, and utility > threshold, so not removed. But later? Should n2 be reconsidered? For now, it's not removed. Since it's a leaf but utility > threshold, it stays. Visit_order: [n4,n5,n6,n1,n2].\n\nProcess n3: removable=false, skip. Visit_order: ... n3.\n\nProcess root: not removable, skip.\n\nEnd. Retained: all except n4, n6. That matches expected? We don't have expected answer, but seems plausible.\n\nBut note: When n2 became leaf, we didn't enqueue it again because it was already in queue. But the algorithm should reconsider it after it becomes leaf. It already is in queue, so it will be processed. That's fine.\n\nHowever what about case where removal of child makes parent leaf, but parent was already processed earlier? For example, if processing order had n2 before n4? But our queue order is depth descending, so children processed before parents. So parents are processed after all their children (if children at deeper depth). But what if depth is equal? In our tree, depth2 children processed first, then depth1 parents. So parents will be processed after all children. But if a child removal happens, the parent is already in queue later, so it will be processed after child removal. That's good. However if a parent has multiple children and some are removed earlier, when we process parent, it will have no retained children and be a leaf. But we already enqueued parent initially. So it will be considered.\n\nBut what about more complex scenario where after processing parent (and not removing because utility > threshold or not removable), later a child removal occurs from another branch? That child removal might make parent leaf? But parent is already processed. Then parent should be reconsidered. In our algorithm, when we remove that child, we enqueue the parent again (if not pending). Since parent was already popped and is not pending, it will be added to queue again. That's correct. So the algorithm should work.\n\nBut careful: The initial queue order processes all nodes. Some nodes may be removed during processing, causing parents to become leaf. Those parents are either not yet processed (if they are deeper? but parents are at shallower depth, so they will be processed after children, so they are still in queue) or already processed (if we had multiple levels? e.g., a node at depth2 removed, its parent at depth1 is not yet processed, so fine; later if a node at depth1 is removed, its parent at depth0 is already processed? root is depth0 and processed last, so it's still in queue. Actually root is last in queue due to depth0, so it's processed after all depth1 nodes. So root will be processed after all potential removals. So root may become leaf after children removal, and it's still in queue. So all good.\n\nNow about output: retained_node_ids list. Should we include root? Yes, root is retained. So list all ids not removed. The visit_order includes all nodes visited, in order of processing.\n\nMake sure to use standard library only.\n\nPotential issues: duplicate enqueue handling: we use pending set to avoid adding duplicate. But when we pop, we remove from pending. So after a node is popped, it's not pending. If later a child removal triggers re-enqueue, it will be added. That's correct.\n\nBut one nuance: When we process a node and it is removable and we remove it, we enqueue parent. But what if the parent is already in queue and not yet processed? We skip adding duplicate. That is fine because the parent will be processed later. However, by the time it is processed, the child removal already happened. So the parent will see that it has no retained children (since child removed). So it's okay. The duplicate would not change outcome.\n\nNow code. We need to encode Python source as JSON string. The entire response must be a JSON object with key \"implementation\". We'll produce the JSON.\n\nWe must follow exact contract: no extra keys, no markdown. So output:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    retained = set(nodes.keys())\\n    sorted_nodes = sorted(nodes.values(), key=lambda n: (-n['depth'], n['id']))\\n    queue = [n['id'] for n in sorted_nodes]\\n    pending = set(queue)\\n    visit_order = []\\n    while queue:\\n        nid = queue.pop(0)\\n        pending.discard(nid)\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        node = nodes[nid]\\n        visit_order.append(nid)\\n        children = [c for c in nodes.values() if c['parent'] == nid and c['id'] in retained]\\n        is_leaf = len(children) == 0\\n        if node['removable'] and node['utility'] <= threshold and is_leaf:\\n            retained.remove(nid)\\n            parent_id = node['parent']\\n            if parent_id is not None and parent_id in retained and parent_id not in pending:\\n                pending.add(parent_id)\\n                queue.append(parent_id)\\n    return {\\\"retained_node_ids\\\": sorted(list(retained)), \\\"visit_order\\\": visit_order}\"}\n\nBut we need to be careful: the queue.pop(0) is O(n) but fine for small. Could use collections.deque but we can keep as is. Python list pop(0) is O(n) each but acceptable.\n\nAlso note: We used sorted for retained_node_ids. The spec doesn't require sorted, but it's safe.\n\nNow we must ensure that the solve function returns JSON-serializable object. It does.\n\nNow we need to produce the final JSON response with the implementation key. The value must be a JSON string (escaped). The entire response is a JSON object.\n\nLet's write it properly with escaped newlines and quotes.\n\nWe'll produce the string.\n\nBut wait: The input case payload may have keys like \"_case_nonce\" which we ignore. We only use nodes and utility_threshold. That's fine.\n\nPotential improvement: The children list computed each time is O(n^2) worst-case if we iterate all nodes for each pop. But n is small. Could precompute children adjacency and update when removing. For correctness, we can do that. Let's implement adjacency map: children_map = {nid: [] for nid in nodes}. Then populate. When removing node, we remove it from its parent's children list. But we also need to get children list quickly. Let's improve:\n\n- Build children_map = {nid: set() for nid in nodes}\n- For each node, add its id to children_map[parent] if parent not None.\n- When removing node, we need to remove it from parent's children set. So we can do: parent_id = node['parent']; if parent_id: children_map[parent_id].discard(nid)\n- Then to check if node is leaf: is_leaf = len(children_map[nid]) == 0\n- For initial queue, we need to sort, but children_map doesn't affect.\n\nThat's more efficient.\n\nAlso note: The removal of node should also remove it from retained set, but we also need to remove it from children_map? Not necessary for leaf check, as we only check children_map for currently retained children? Actually children_map contains all children initially. We need to keep it updated: when we remove a child, we should remove it from its parent's children map. So we need to update children_map accordingly. Alternatively, we can compute children on the fly by filtering nodes by parent and checking retained. That's simpler but less efficient. Given small input, it's fine. But to avoid potential misinterpretation, we can use the simple approach. The specification doesn't require optimal performance. I'll use the simple method as it's straightforward.\n\nNow ensure we handle case where node has no parent (root). Parent is null or None? In payload, root's parent is null. So we check node['parent'] is not None.\n\nNow final code.\n\nWe must output the exact JSON. Let's write it.\n\nBut wait: The task says \"Treat these registered properties as hard contracts rather than soft preferences: no_invalidated_revisit, queue_order, normalized_output, termination.\" So we must ensure no invalidated revisit: we only revisit when parent is enqueued after child removal. Our algorithm does that. Queue order: initial descending depth then id. That's correct. Normalized output: retained_node_ids and visit_order. Termination: loop ends when queue empty.\n\nSeems fine.\n\nNow write the response.\n\nWe'll produce the JSON string with escaped quotes and newlines.\n\nLet's write:\n\n{\"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = {n['id']: n for n in case['nodes']}\\n    threshold = case['utility_threshold']\\n    retained = set